---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import LeadForm from '../../components/LeadForm.astro';
import { SITE } from '../../config/site';
import { breadcrumbSchema, faqSchema } from '../../lib/schema';

const title = 'Home renovation loans in Toronto: HELP, and what else actually exists | AuditYourHome';
const description =
  'Financing a Toronto home renovation in 2026: the City\'s HELP program (up to $125,000 at 3.34%–4.75%), why the federal Greener Homes Loan is gone, the CMHC Eco 25% refund, and how an energy assessment unlocks the best terms.';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What is the Home Energy Loan Program (HELP)?',
    a: 'Toronto\'s City-run financing for home energy retrofits: up to $125,000 (or 10% of your property\'s assessed value, whichever is less), at posted fixed rates of 3.34% (5-year) to 4.75% (20-year, valid to Sept 30, 2026) plus a 2% administrative charge. It\'s repaid through your property tax bill, and most projects require an EnerGuide home energy assessment first.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is the Canada Greener Homes Loan still available?',
    a: 'No. The federal interest-free loan ($5,000–$40,000) closed to new applications on October 1, 2025, when its funding was fully committed. Many websites still advertise it — treat that as a sign the rest of their information is stale too. For Toronto homeowners, HELP is the closest living equivalent.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does HELP stay with me or the house if I sell?',
    a: 'HELP is structured as a local improvement charge tied to the property and repaid via the property tax bill — an arrangement your lawyer and lender should review at sale or refinance time. Toronto\'s program pages cover the mechanics; ask the program team about discharge and assumption details before signing.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I stack HELP with rebates?',
    a: 'Yes — that\'s the play. A common Toronto sequence: EnerGuide assessment → HELP financing for the retrofit → Home Renovation Savings rebates on insulation ($7,700 max), windows, and heat pumps → the $600 assessment rebate → and for CMHC-insured recent buyers, the 25% Eco Improvement premium refund on top.',
  },
];

const schema = [
  breadcrumbSchema([
    { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
    { name: 'Articles', path: '/articles/' },
    { name: 'Home renovation loans in Toronto', path: '/articles/home-renovation-loans-toronto/' },
  ]),
  faqSchema(faqs),
];
---

<Base title={title} description={description} schema={schema}>
  <div class="container">
    <nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/articles/">Articles</a> <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> <span>Home renovation loans in Toronto</span></nav>
    <div class="page-hero">
      <p class="eyebrow">Article · verified {SITE.dataReviewDate}</p>
      <h1>Home renovation loans in Toronto: HELP, and what else actually exists</h1>
      <p class="lede measure">
        Search for renovation financing in Toronto and you'll find a graveyard of closed federal
        programs still being advertised. Here's what's actually alive in 2026 — led by the City
        program most homeowners have never heard of — and how an energy assessment turns borrowing
        into stacked rebates.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="measure prose">
      <h2>The one that's alive: Toronto's HELP</h2>
      <p>
        The <strong>Home Energy Loan Program</strong> is the City of Toronto's own financing for
        energy retrofits — insulation, windows, heat pumps, solar, geothermal — and it's open now
        (<a href="https://www.toronto.ca/services-payments/water-environment/environmental-grants-incentives/home-energy-loan-program-help/" rel="noopener">toronto.ca ↗</a>,
        verified {SITE.dataReviewDate}). The shape of it: up to <strong>$125,000</strong> or 10%
        of your property's assessed value, fixed posted rates currently
        <strong>3.34% (5-year) to 4.75% (20-year)</strong> — the 20-year term reserved for solar,
        geothermal, windows, and heat pumps — plus a 2% administrative charge, repaid through your
        property tax bill. A $50,000 loan over 10 years runs roughly $455/month on the City's own
        example. Most projects require an <strong>EnerGuide home energy assessment</strong> with a
        registered advisor first; heat-pump-only and solar-only projects are exempt.
      </p>

      <h2>The one that's gone (and still haunting Google)</h2>
      <p>
        The federal <strong>Canada Greener Homes Loan</strong> — $40,000, interest-free — closed
        to new applications <strong>October 1, 2025</strong> when its funding was fully committed
        (<a href="https://natural-resources.canada.ca/energy-efficiency/home-energy-efficiency/canada-greener-homes-initiative/canada-greener-homes-loan" rel="noopener">NRCan ↗</a>).
        No replacement was announced in Budget 2025. If a contractor or website is promising it,
        their information is at least a year old — a useful trust test we apply across our
        <a href="/programs/">program tracker</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>The refund that rides along: CMHC Eco Improvement</h2>
      <p>
        If you bought recently with a CMHC-insured mortgage, completing <strong>$20,000+</strong>
        of eligible energy renovations earns a <strong>25% refund of your mortgage insurance
        premium</strong> — applications within 2 years of closing
        (<a href="https://www.cmhc-schl.gc.ca/consumers/home-buying/mortgage-loan-insurance-for-consumers/cmhc-eco-products/cmhc-eco-improvement" rel="noopener">CMHC ↗</a>,
        verified {SITE.dataReviewDate}). On Toronto premiums that's routinely thousands of dollars
        for work you were financing anyway. Sagen and Canada Guaranty offer comparable programs.
      </p>

      <h2>The rest of the toolbox</h2>
      <p>
        Beyond the programs: secured lines of credit, mortgage refinancing, and purchase-plus-improvements
        mortgages are the conventional routes banks offer for renovation money. Which fits depends
        on your rate, equity, and timeline — we're a directory, not a lender, so take the
        comparison to your broker or bank rather than to a website. (Nothing on this page is
        financial advice; it's the program map we verify so you can ask better questions.)
      </p>

      <h2>The sequence that maximizes it</h2>
      <p>
        Toronto's stack rewards a specific order: <strong>book the EnerGuide assessment first</strong>
        — it's the document HELP requires <em>and</em> the key to Home Renovation Savings' bundled
        rebates (insulation up to $7,700, $100 per window opening, the $600 assessment rebate).
        Finance through HELP, collect the rebates against the same work, then claim CMHC Eco if
        you're an insured recent buyer. The <a href="/plan/">planner</a> builds your version of
        this sequence, and the <a href="/find/toronto/">Toronto directory</a> lists every
        registered advisor who can start it.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Common questions</h2>
    <div class="measure">
      {faqs.map((f) => (
        <details class="faq"><summary>{f.q}</summary><div class="faq-body"><p>{f.a}</p></div></details>
      ))}
    </div>

    <div class="card card-pad srcs">
      <h3>Sources</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://www.toronto.ca/services-payments/water-environment/environmental-grants-incentives/home-energy-loan-program-help/" rel="noopener">City of Toronto — HELP (rates &amp; terms, verified {SITE.dataReviewDate})</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://natural-resources.canada.ca/energy-efficiency/home-energy-efficiency/canada-greener-homes-initiative/canada-greener-homes-loan" rel="noopener">NRCan — Canada Greener Homes Loan (closed Oct 1, 2025)</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.cmhc-schl.gc.ca/consumers/home-buying/mortgage-loan-insurance-for-consumers/cmhc-eco-products/cmhc-eco-improvement" rel="noopener">CMHC — Eco Improvement premium refund (verified {SITE.dataReviewDate})</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.homerenovationsavings.ca/with-assessment" rel="noopener">Home Renovation Savings — assessment path (verified {SITE.dataReviewDate})</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <LeadForm heading="Start with the assessment HELP requires" sub="We'll match you with a registered Toronto service organization — the first step for the loan and the rebates alike." region="toronto" context="article-toronto-loans" />
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .crumbs { padding-top: 24px; font-size: 13.5px; color: var(--faint); }
  .crumbs a { color: var(--muted); }
  .prose h2 { font-size: 23px; margin: 26px 0 10px; }
  .srcs { border-left: 4px solid var(--steel); margin: 30px 0 10px; }
  .srcs ul { margin: 0; padding-left: 20px; display: grid; gap: 6px; font-size: 14px; }
</style>
